 "observations": [
  "The page number «141» stands at the head of the page, centred, with a rule below it; at the right the number of the table, «Tab. 30», in bold sans serif, underlined. The page has no signature. A speck at the left above the titles; not transcribed. The titles (L01-L03), in bold, the sigla in roman; L03 in smaller bold type, as on p. 140 (JP2 0172) «3 S. 13 G. 12 M.» followed by «(CO)» in roman. The folios of the manuscripts stand at the right of the last title in two lines, «C 57v, O 115r,» and «N 27r.», the letters raised; here as right margin notes.",
  "The table: rules and heads as on p. 140 (JP2 0172), with «Pagina numerorum (O) Catur eladed (C)» over the four columns of the arguments, divided below a rule into «Augmentum proprietatum omnium1)» and «Diminutio proprietatum omnium1)» in smaller type; «Sublimatio ascensus Saturni examinata2)»; «Examinatio ipsius elheza3)» and «Examinatio centri», each hyphenated «Exami-natio»; «Statio prima Saturni»; «Latitudo Saturni recedentis4)» over «prima» and «secunda» in smaller bold type, «recedentis» because the latitude decreases after 90°. Here each head is set from the top, one printed line to a line (L04-L08), the cells separated by our «|»; a head followed by empty cells spans their columns. L09, the units, in bold.",
  "L10-L39. Checked as on p. 138 (JP2 0170): the Sublimatio is 8s 4° 55′ less half the Examinatio argumenti of the same row in every row, a half minute counted as a whole. The Latitudo secunda is 5° · sin x to within 0.7″ in every row, and the value for 90° + y is that for 90° − y on pp. 139-140 (JP2 0171-0172) in every row. The Examinatio argumenti reaches its greatest value, 5° 44′, from 95° to 98°: the greatest equation of an epicycle of radius 6, arc sin 1/10 = 5° 44.4′ at 95.7°. Against the equation of that epicycle the table is up to 1.2′ smaller to 103° and up to 2.5′ larger from 104° on, 5° 15′ at 120° for 5° 12.5′ computed.",
  "L10-L39, the other columns: the Examinatio centri for 90° + y is that for 90° − y on pp. 139-140 (JP2 0171-0172) in every row except at 100°, 8° 24′, where 80° has 8° 25′, and at 102°, 8° 21′, where 78° has 8° 22′; the steps from 99° to 103° are 2′, 1′, 2′, 1′, where the corresponding steps from 81° back to 77° are 1′, 2′, 1′, 2′. The Statio prima increases from 3s 24° 12′ to 3s 24° 50′ by 1′ or 2′ a degree. The Latitudo prima is 1° 18′ to 92°, 1° 17′ to 97°, 1° 16′ to 106° and 1° 15′ from 107° on; it is not symmetric about 90° and continues to decrease from the values of p. 140.",
  "Suter's examples. The Sublimatio 242° 5′ for the anomaly 104° 28′ (p. 53, JP2 0085, L14) is the 8s 2° 5′ of L23 (104°). The Examinatio argumenti 5° 35′ 35″ for the argumentum examinatum 109° 24′ 30″ (L24 there) is the value interpolated between 5° 36′ (109°, L28) and 5° 35′ (110°, L29), 5° 35′ 35.5″. The latitudo prima 1° 15′ for 109° 24½′ (p. 62, JP2 0094, L34) is that of L28.",
  "Notes on the readings. Checked: the editor's readings agree with the rows around them where the variants depart: the Examinatio centri 8° 34′ at 92° (note b, C), the value at 88°; the Latitudo prima in notes c, d and e, 1° 15′ at 107° (C) against the 16 of O and N; the Latitudo secunda 4° 56′ 18″, 4° 27′ 18″, 4° 48′ 23″ and 4° 46′ 53″ (notes f, g, h, i), 4° 56′ 18.4″, 4° 27′ 18.1″, 4° 48′ 22.7″ and 4° 46′ 53.3″ computed.",
  "Italics: «om.». N01: the ellipses «. . . .» of the lemmata as printed. L05, «Catur», L09, «Min» without its point in the units of the Examinatio argumenti, and L11, «34b» without the parenthesis of the note mark: see the critical notes."
 ],
